If a Scott map φ is way below a Scott map g, then φ lies below a finite join of step maps whose second components are way below g at the first components.

The finite joins of such step maps form a nonempty directed family: the empty join is included, and the union of two admissible finite sets is admissible, with a larger join. The supremum of the family is g, because each step map lies below g, while at each argument the value of g is the supremum of values of step maps that belong to the family. Since φ is way below this supremum, it is way below one member of the family.
